  "headline": "Lease disclosures leave 2027 rack acceptance unresolved; NVIDIA base held at $374.62bn",
  "note": "I found primary buildout windows, but no matched power-ready and rack-acceptance schedule. Microsoft’s FY2026 10-K explicitly warns about power-connection delays; its lease note spans FY2027–33. Alphabet’s Q2 note spans 2026–31 and names a separate Q3 2026 lease commencement. Meta’s July leases are expected to start in 2027–28 [web-454ed8f95a] [web-7e3bfccf0e] [web-7e69fd6db3] [web-b8b374a1b1]. These disclosures predate the channel interviews and independently document infrastructure exposure; they cannot identify its unnamed planner or prove a slip [channel-oct2026].\n\nAmazon’s energy contracts can settle financially instead of delivering electricity when consumption is lower. Their existence does not establish site-level 2027 power readiness [web-c22fb8e0b1]. Alphabet’s lease prepayments also show that cash can precede commencement. Neither lease commitments nor payment maturities are replacement capex guides.\n\nNVIDIA recognizes product revenue when control transfers. The policy supplies no customer-specific acceptance calendar. Its Ohio/OpenAI guarantee disclosure puts the first construction phase in FY2029, so that project cannot substantiate 2027 capacity [web-b93e425ae9] [web-86d7fcc726].\n\nI retain $682.5bn calendar capex, NVIDIA $374.62bn base/$391.79bn bull, Q3 Data Center $103bn and own Q4 $128bn. Move on a dated site or acceptance delay linked to supplier dollars, not a lease total. Late-October customer results and November supplier commentary are the next tests. NVIDIA’s long timing risk is better documented; its base street gap remains +$2.62bn (+0.70%), and AMD’s remains approximately zero.",
  "stance": "Capex remains modestly above street; primary filings document power risk without a dated supplier delay that warrants cutting NVIDIA’s base.",
  "rationale": "The reviewed primary disclosures provide future lease windows, energy-contract terms and general revenue-recognition policy. None links an identified site’s power-ready capacity to a specific rack’s transfer-of-control date and supplier value. These July/February/August disclosures corroborate the mechanism behind the channel concern, not the anonymous observation or a new 2026 slip. Hold all inputs and quarters; retain the previous 65.5% NVIDIA bull share discount. A quantified schedule change affecting delivery or acceptance, or revised capex guidance, would move the forecast.",
  "thesis": "Calendar 2026 capex stays $682.5bn versus $667.5bn synthetic street. NVIDIA Data Center bear/base/bull remains $351.34bn/$374.62bn/$391.79bn, with base $2.62bn above $372bn consensus; AMD GPU base stays $14.00bn versus $14bn. Mix remains 37%/38%/39%; NVIDIA split 64%/65%/65.5%, AMD split 2.5%/2.7%/3%; NVIDIA Big Four concentration 46%/45%/44.5%, AMD 55%/50%/45%. Primary lease schedules do not certify energized MW or vendor acceptance dates. Cash, lease commencement, delivery/control transfer and energization can differ. Q3 FY27 NVIDIA Data Center stays $103bn, expected November 18 unconfirmed; own Q4 stays $128bn versus $129.86bn required. FY27 Q4 has 14 weeks, while the official calendar bridge retains its stated monthly convention.",
